        "## Overview & Intuition\n",
        "\n",
        "We examine characteristic functions (Fourier transforms) of probability measures $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$. \n",
        "\n",
        "* If $\\mu$ is an **absolutely continuous** probability measure with respect to Lebesgue measure $\\lambda_d$, so that $d\\mu = f \\, d\\lambda_d$ with $f \\in L^1(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, then its characteristic function $\\hat{f}(\\xi) \\to 0$ as $\\|\\xi\\| \\to \\infty$.\n",
        "* By contrast, a purely atomic probability measure need not have a characteristic function that decays to zero at infinity. For example, $\\frac12(\\delta_1+\\delta_{-1})$ has characteristic function $\\cos(\\xi)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Characteristic Functions of Selected Distributions\n",
        "\n",
        "Below are four classic characteristic functions representing both continuous and discrete probability distributions:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Uniform Distribution**: If $X$ is uniform on $[-1,1]$, its density is $f(x)=\\frac12\\mathbf{1}_{[-1,1]}(x)$ and its characteristic function is the sinc function:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat{f}(\\xi) =\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "\\dfrac{\\sin(\\xi)}{\\xi}, & \\xi\\ne0,\\\\\n",
        "1, & \\xi=0.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$"
      ],
